/*!
   \qmlmodule org.kde.kirigamiaddons.formcard
   \title Kirigami Addons FormCard QML Types
   \brief Form controls for settings pages and dialogs.

   The FormCard module provides controls for settings pages and other forms.
   Import it with an alias so that its types are easy to distinguish from
   regular Qt Quick Controls:

   \qml
   import org.kde.kirigamiaddons.formcard as FormCard
   \endqml

   \section1 Organizing a form

   A \l FormCardPage is a scrollable page that arranges form sections. Each
   section normally has a \l FormHeader followed by a \l FormCard. Put the
   header beside the card in the page, not inside it. The card holds one or
   more form delegates, such as \l FormSwitchDelegate,
   \l FormSliderDelegate, \l FormTextFieldDelegate, or \l FormButtonDelegate. The card lays out its
   delegates vertically and supplies their shared background.

   \qml
   import org.kde.kirigamiaddons.formcard as FormCard

   FormCard.FormCardPage {
       title: i18nc("@title", "Settings")

       FormCard.FormHeader {
           title: i18nc("@title:group", "Account")
       }

       FormCard.FormCard {
           autoSeparators: true

           FormCard.FormTextFieldDelegate {
               label: i18nc("@label:textbox", "Display name")
           }

           FormCard.FormSwitchDelegate {
               text: i18nc("@option:check", "Show online status")
           }
       }
   }
   \endqml

   Add another header and card for another section. The card's
   \l FormCard::autoSeparators property inserts separators between visible
   delegates when enabled. You can also add \l FormDelegateSeparator
   explicitly when you need to control where a separator appears.

   \section1 Creating a custom delegate

   Use \l AbstractFormDelegate as the root of a reusable QML component. It
   supplies the card's padding, hover and click behavior, and default
   background. Provide a \c contentItem with an implicit size, and expose
   any additional properties your delegate needs. For example, save the
   following as \c CounterDelegate.qml:

   \qml
   import QtQuick.Controls as Controls
   import org.kde.kirigamiaddons.formcard as FormCard

   FormCard.AbstractFormDelegate {
       id: root

       property int count: 0

       text: i18nc("@action:button", "Count: %1").arg(count)
       onClicked: root.count++

       contentItem: Controls.Label {
           text: root.text
       }
   }
   \endqml

   Use \c CounterDelegate inside a \l FormCard, just like the built-in
   delegates. The inherited \c text property also gives this clickable
   delegate an accessible name. To remove the default background for a
   different design, set \c {background: null}.

   \section1 Using delegates in a dialog

   \l FormCardDialog provides a dialog header, a vertical layout for form
   delegates, and optional standard buttons. Place delegates directly inside
   it; no \l FormCard or \l FormCardPage is needed. Use \c standardButtons to
   choose the actions and handle \c onAccepted or \c onRejected as with a
   Qt Quick Controls Dialog:

   \qml
   import QtQuick.Controls as Controls
   import org.kde.kirigamiaddons.formcard as FormCard

   FormCard.FormCardDialog {
       id: dialog

       signal profileCreated(string name)

       title: i18nc("@title:window", "Create Profile")
       standardButtons: Controls.Dialog.Ok | Controls.Dialog.Cancel

       FormCard.FormTextFieldDelegate {
           id: nameField
           label: i18nc("@label:textbox", "Profile name")
       }

       FormCard.FormDelegateSeparator {}

       FormCard.FormSwitchDelegate {
           text: i18nc("@option:check", "Use as default")
       }

       onAccepted: profileCreated(nameField.text)
   }
   \endqml

   Call \c dialog.open() from the action that should show the form. Custom
   delegates based on \l AbstractFormDelegate can also be placed directly
   in a \l FormCardDialog.

   \sa FormCardPage
   \sa FormCard
   \sa FormCardDialog
   \sa AbstractFormDelegate
 */
